@startuml

title Classifying a set of data: relatedness decides the bundle, provenance the package

skinparam activity {
    BackgroundColor #F7F7F7
    BorderColor #555555
    DiamondBackgroundColor #FFFFFF
    DiamondBorderColor #555555
    FontSize 13
}

skinparam ArrowColor #555555

start

:A file, a screen or a workflow step names a set of records;

:Step 1: name the data, its caller and its operation;

:Step 3: do the families share an invariant at one instant,\nor one operation whose intent is all-or-nothing?;

if () then (no)
  :There is no relatedness to carry.\nSplit the set;
  :Is the set one entity kind?;
  if () then (yes)
    :**List**\none typed array, paged, a total;
  else (no)
    :**Entity** per kind, or **List** per collection;
    note right
      If the bytes must still move as one
      artefact, that artefact is a **Package**.
    end note
  endif
else (yes)
  :Is the only thing joining the set\na file, a screen or a workflow step?;
  if () then (yes)
    :There is no relatedness, only a format.\nThe file is an **Artefact**;
    :Split it, and classify what it converts into;
  else (no)
    :Step 4: does one owner hold every family?;
    if () then (one owner)
      :**Bundle**\nrelated families, one unit_of_work\nthe largest atomic thing;
    else (several owners)
      :**Package**\none bundle per owner, dispatched\nnever atomic across bundles or owners;
    endif
  endif
endif

:Steps 6-9: name the sanctioned pattern and the\ngenerated artefact, the atomicity scope,\nthe consistency point and what is forbidden;

note right
  A hand-written union of unrelated entities
  fits none of these. It is refused, and it is
  reported as a missing classification.
end note

stop

@enduml
